\title{RLVM agentic journal}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Working notes for RLVM (Rabdos Lean VM), a checker for the Lean 4 kernel on GraalVM. Agents and the maintainer record design notes, decisions, measured findings, open problems, examples and dated changelog entries here as small trees that link to each other. A short tree states one point; the long research, design and review documents are trees of their own, the studies, the reports and the full design documents, and are linked from the short ones. The journal is documentation under the GNU Free Documentation License; see the [licence](licence).}

\subtree[index-what]{
\title{What RLVM is}

\p{RLVM reads an export of a Lean 4 library in the format of \code{lean4export} and answers accept, reject or decline, as an external checker for the Lean Kernel Arena does. It is written in Java for GraalVM and runs as a native image or on the JVM.}

\p{The maintainer's framing is that the project is a \em{mechanisation} of the kernel's type checking in the sense of type theory: the judgments of the kernel (inference, checking, conversion, reduction to weak-head normal form) are entries of a worklist with continuations, and RLVM is the machine that steps that worklist. Its design is written as an instruction set for normalisation by evaluation and definitional equality. The goal is real checking performance, which means warm wall-clock time with parallel workers, with the verdicts of the official checker. Both are stated in the [standing policies](standing-policies).}

\p{There are two engines behind one pipeline of loader, store, admission and driver. The \em{reference engine} is a port of the official algorithm over a hash-consed term store: it admits inductive types, re-checks every rejection, is the oracle of the differential tests and is the strict mode. The \em{machine} is the worklist engine on values that is meant to carry the checking work.}
}

\subtree[index-state]{
\title{Where it stands}

\p{State at the end of 2026-11-24, the second day of the project, on branch \code{dev}.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Verdicts.} The pipeline accepts all eight corpora it is measured on (Init, Std, cedar, cslib, con-leche, reachability, navier-stokes-euler and Mathlib) and gives the expected verdict on the 230 published arena tests without a decline. On 17,775 mutated exports with stored official verdicts it has no wrong accept and no wrong reject. On the 1,196 hostile exports of the reviews, each with the verdict of the official checker, it has no wrong accept and no wrong reject either; 68 of them are accepts of the tolerated kind, see [accepted risks](accepted-risks).}
\li{\strong{Default engine.} The machine is the default engine since the evening of 2026-11-24, with a default switch set that includes eager reduction and the two proof-comparison shortcuts; the reference engine re-checks every rejection and decides every unit the machine declines, and alone it is the strict policy. The machine had been the default for under an hour that afternoon and was taken back when new hostile exports showed accepts that the official checker rejects. Those were fixed or classified, the maintainer chose practical exactness, an adversarial review of the candidate found no accept of an underivable statement, and the switch set was chosen by warm wall clock over the eight corpora. See [the decision on the default engine](dec-T79V) and [the decision on the default switch set](dec-YP0N).}
\li{\strong{Release.} One executable, the native image, whose plain run is the default configuration; the JVM launcher is a tool of the repository, and a profile-guided build with Oracle GraalVM is an option of the build. See [packaging](design-packaging).}
\li{\strong{Speed.} Warm wall clock with 8 workers and pipelined loading, on the aarch64 development host, from [the benchmark tables](rep-benchmarks) of the released set of jars. On all of Mathlib the executable in its default configuration takes 15.46 s per warm pass and 16.31 s as a cold process; the same configuration on the JVM launcher takes 12.44 s per warm pass. On the same eight cores, in windows of their own, the two leading checkers of the arena take 14.20 s (gruszka) and 18.66 s (nano-lean); the reference engine by name takes 85.76 s as a cold process. The executable is at 1.00, 0.95 and 1.05 times gruszka's warm time on Init, Std and cedar, at 0.23 on reachability, and at 1.35, 1.37 and 1.24 on con-leche, navier-stokes-euler and cslib. In the arena's metric, the instructions of a cold process, it needs 3.5 to 4.1 times gruszka's count on seven corpora and 1.23 times on reachability. Nothing was measured on x86-64, the architecture of the arena's runner. The session trees quote the tables of earlier states, which were taken with other distributions in other windows.}
\li{\strong{Where the gap is.} In the afternoon of 2026-11-24 the profiles of Mathlib attributed most of the distance to gruszka to the official rule for comparing proofs, which the leading checkers do not follow, and to declarations that enter eager reduction; both are handled by the default switch set now. What remains is a uniform cost per operation, see [the problem tree](prob-28KQ), and in the native image the collector; see also [the gap to gruszka](prob-TJK3) and [what the release leaves open](prob-HC9J).}
\li{\strong{Notes.} Since the maintainer's rule of 2026-11-24 every note of the project lives in this journal; the long documents that were under \code{docs/} and beside the modules are trees here, the [studies](studies), the [reports](reports) and the full design documents.}
}
}

\subtree[index-hubs]{
\title{Contents}

\ul{
\li{[Design notes](designs): how each part is meant to work and why, in a reading order.}
\li{[Decisions](decisions): what was chosen, against which alternatives, and what follows from it.}
\li{[Insights](insights): measured findings, with their numbers and conditions, and hypotheses not yet measured.}
\li{[Reports](reports): the full record of each measurement campaign, review and spike.}
\li{[Studies](studies): readings of outside sources - the arena, the official kernel, other checkers, techniques.}
\li{[Where the documents went](moved-documents): the table from the path of a former document to its tree.}
\li{[Open problems](open-problems): what is not settled, what is known, and what would close it.}
\li{[Examples](examples): inputs that show a rule of the kernel or a wrong verdict.}
\li{[Changelog](changelog): dated entries, by month and [by area](release-unreleased).}
\li{[Sessions](sessions): one tree per phase of the work, with what was done and where it is recorded.}
\li{[Standing policies](standing-policies): the maintainer's lasting instructions, with their dates.}
\li{[The owner's notes](owner-notes): the maintainer's instructions 1 to 16 as they were given, each with its reading.}
\li{[Accepted risks](accepted-risks): trade-offs decided on purpose, among them where the default configuration may differ from the official checker.}
\li{[People and agents](agents): who wrote what.}
\li{[Licence](licence).}
}
}
